; bfsodium HKDF_SHA_512 : RFC 5869
;
; HAND WRITTEN skeleton; The keyed hash is sha512/hmac  PASTED  and entered once
; to extract and once per sixty four bytes of output to expand;
;
; As in sha512/hashcore  a run of ONE repeated token is a repetition rather
; than a decision and is written as such; every offset and every branch below
; was worked out from the map and written down;
;
; IO  in:  saltlen{2} LE  ikmlen{2} LE  infolen{2} LE  okmlen{2} LE
;          salt{256}  ikm{256}  info{256}
;     out: okm{okmlen}
;
; Only the first saltlen bytes of the salt buffer are looked at  and the same
; for the other two; the rest MUST be nought;
;
; INFOLEN IS AT MOST SIXTY THREE  and that is a real limit worth stating; the
; message expand hands to HMAC is T then info then the counter byte  T is sixty
; four bytes  and sha512/hmac takes at most a hundred and twenty eight bytes
; from memory because sha512/hashcore's prefix buffer is two hundred and fifty
; six and the pad block takes half of it; sixty four plus sixty three plus one
; is a hundred and twenty eight; The SHA_256 file has the same limit at a
; hundred and ninety two for the same reason; Widening it means widening
; hashcore's prefix buffer  which moves every cell above it  so it is a change
; to make deliberately and not in passing;
;
; TAPE MAP  (home @0)
;   @0x000:0x007  the four lengths  each two bytes little endian
;   @0x008:0x107  saltbuf{256}
;   @0x108:0x207  ikmbuf{256}
;   @0x208:0x307  infobuf{256}
;   @0x310:0xd7f  sha512/hmac's frame  pasted at @0x310; footprint 0:2671
;   @0xd88:0xdc7  prk{64}   the pseudorandom key extract makes
;   @0xdd0:0xe0f  T{64}     the block expand is on  and the next message's head
;   @0xe18:0xe57  a sixty four byte temp  which hands the key and T back
;   @0xe60:0xe6d  the counter  the count still owed  T's length and the slide
;                 count  with the flags the counts are tested through
;   @0xe74:0xe8f  the adder frame; add64 footprint 0:27 at @0xe74
;   @0xeb0:0xeef  a sixty four byte temp  which hands info back after a copy
;   @0xf20:0xf5f  a sixty four byte copy of T  which is written out and spent
;
; RFC 5869 is two halves; extract makes a pseudorandom key from the salt and the
; input keying material  and expand stretches it to any length by chaining
;   T{i} = HMAC( prk  T{i minus 1}  info  i )
; and running the T values together;
;
; THE COUNTER IS PLACED BY SLIDING  NOT BY COMPUTING AN ADDRESS; it belongs
; after T and info  whose combined length is only known at run time  and this
; library has no index; so the byte is dropped on the top of an empty message
; buffer and the whole buffer slides down until it is where it belongs; info and
; T are then ADDED underneath it  which cannot disturb it because the bytes of
; each beyond its own length are nought; The slide count starts at two hundred
; and fifty five  which is one short of the buffer  and info's length and T's
; are taken off it;
;
; Nothing buffers the output; each T is written as it is made and the last is
; cut short  so the tape does not grow with the length asked for;
;
; A digest is a byte string and hmac leaves its answer in H as words  so every
; path that carries one out reverses each word; that is the same boundary
; sha512/hmac documents and gets wrong at its peril; hmac's own H sits at 1564
; because its hashcore is pasted at 1304 and a hashcore keeps H 260 above its
; base;
;
; THE SAME SHAPE AS sha256/hkdf  section for section; T is sixty four bytes
; rather than thirty two  the key padding block is a hundred and twenty eight
; and the info allowance is sixty three rather than a hundred and ninety two;

; read the four lengths and the three buffers
,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,
